          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Series Paintings</strong><br>\nMonet's later years were marked by his famous series paintings, in which he captured the same scene under different lighting conditions and seasons, such as his \"Haystacks,\" \"Rouen Cathedral,\" and \"Water Lilies\" series.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Water Lilies and Giverny</strong><br>\nMonet's property in Giverny, where he moved in 1883, became the subject of his renowned \"Water Lilies\" series. His garden and the pond with a Japanese bridge provided endless inspiration for his works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Innovations in Art</strong><br>\nMonet's approach to painting outdoors and \"en plein air\" was innovative, enabling him to portray the transient effects of weather and time on landscapes more effectively.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
